A||||2||DaveL||EGR  87 2.5 DIS||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||08-14-2000||08:54 AM||||The Egr valve on my 2.5 is very old and rusty, and I really doubt that it has ever been off the engine. I get this light knock during neutral throttle pressure, meaning not idling or not full throttle. If I use 87 octane it knocks like crazy. 93 oct minimizes it. Anyway, I wrote the OEM number off the old valve and went to Autozone and got a new one. It says POS on the top, and according to the directions included, you can use pos. and neg. backpressure egr valves for the same apps. Does this sound right? The OEM is 17075673. Below that on the original valve it says 120  71N. What I got was a Wells brand, number 123. There is inside the box a set of different size metal washers, and a list that tells you which one to use for different OEM numbers. According to this I need the one marked 40. The other question I have is that being the old one is so rusty looking, is there any danger of breaking off the bolt heads when I try removing them? If that happens, I'll be stuck halfway through the job. I've heard of something called an easy-out, but have never used one.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||08-14-2000||11:46 AM||||Dave... Been there with my 88. Failed smog.<BR>Also had the spark rattle at "steady cruise". EGR valve fixed both. <BR>Replacement *was* a bit confusing, though. Just follow the directions. It should work fine. You should "stake" the washer into the bottom of the EGR valve. As for the rusty fasteners, spray on a bunch of Liquid Wrench, and let it soak for a while. It has worked well for me. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>When I die, I want to go peacefully, in my sleep, like Grandpa.<BR>Not screaming, like the passengers in his car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-14-2000||02:45 PM||||Actually EGR replacement likely won't solve your problem... Been there, done that. <P>There is a replacement PROM for the ECM. There is a minor timing issue with the originals. You need to hook the ECM up to a scanner (NOT a code retriever) and get the ID number out of the prom.<P>You then need Table 2 from GM TSB 89-6-65 "Multiple Driveability Concerns, for 2.5L engine (Installation of new PROM)"<P>This TSB has all the part numbers for the new PROMS, BUT YOU MUST KNOW THE ID# of the existing prom. That requires an ECM scanner, as it is NOT printed on the PROM.<P>I've found on mine that having a 180 thermostat and enabling the fan in hot weather solves allot of the problem, but it still does it at times. Depends what gas I use. (It's all 89 but it's crabby about the brands.)<P>No, the bolts likely won't break... steel bolts into aluminum intake. You'll strip the holes if anything. Then you'll need some small Helicoil inserts. Just follow the directions that come with them.<P>Make sure you crimp the edge if the area where the washer sits into the new EGR. Failing to do so could cost you the engine if/when the washer falls out. Crimp at least 3 points around the washer with a center punch.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-14-2000).]||||reg||1
